  "assessment": {
    "rationale": "The specimen is directly relevant because payload interpretation is explicitly pCID-gated: Bob can parse the outer CBOR array and recover the pCID locally, but chunk verification and fetch planning still depend on resolving the pCID's handler or spec. It scores for clean boundaries and explicit unsupported handling, but unsigned v0 and protocol-availability dependence weaken durability and auditability.",
    "strengths": [
      "Deterministic CBOR [pcid, payload] lets Bob recover the governing pCID and raw bytes locally.",
      "The envelope/payload boundary is explicit, so interpretation failures happen at a clear dispatch point.",
      "Unknown-pCID handling requires unsupported and unverified labeling instead of pretending success."
    ],
    "weaknesses": [
      "Chunk verification and fetch planning remain blocked until the pCID's protocol or handler is resolved.",
      "The envelope exposes no generic chunk-manifest or verification hints outside the pCID-specific payload.",
      "Unsigned v0 leaves authenticity, integrity, and accountability outside the base envelope."
    ],
    "risks": [
      "Long-lived objects can become effectively stranded if the governing protocol spec or handler is unavailable.",
      "Best-effort salvage may invite misleading partial interpretations of payload bytes.",
      "Externalizing integrity to transport or payload protocols creates uneven audit guarantees across carriers."
    ],
    "open_questions": [
      "Should object roots expose fetch-plan or chunk-verification hints outside pCID-specific payload semantics?",
      "How are pCID specs and handlers discovered and preserved for 100-year use without a central registry?",
      "Is unsigned v0 acceptable for pCID-driven object roots, or should integrity wrappers be mandatory?"
    ],
    "authority_boundary": "Evidence only; does not settle PromiseGrid design."
  }
